WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.100
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Heath Drazen, who's creator and host

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.880
of the podcast Extremely American, produced
in partnership with post-Industrial Media

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.440
and Boise State Public Radio.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.450
You can find extremely American everywhere
you get podcasts.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.490
Heath, it's really great having you on today.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:18.520
I appreciate it.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.710
Speaker 4: Great to be here, thanks.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:25.821
Speaker 1: So, you know, it certainly since
the Trump riots and even before that, we've

00:00:25.821 --> 00:00:31.949
been covering militia groups, so-called militia
groups for for a long period of time.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:38.200
You covered the Pennsylvania volunteer militia,
which is sort of adjacent to some of the other

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.110
groups, maybe people in my audience are familiar
with.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.080
Can you talk a little bit about how did you
get interested in this?

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.910
How did you start covering them?

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:46.960
What are some of their views?

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.780
How would we characterize these groups?

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:55.810
Speaker 4: Yeah, I mean, I've been covering
the militia movement and kind of the broader,

00:00:55.810 --> 00:01:00.010
far right movement for a number of years now.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:03.860
What kind of got me started on this podcast
is I started seeing them get a lot more politically

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:09.800
active kind of come out of the words come
out of the mountains and be much more visible

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.930
in everyday life.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:17.580
The Pennsylvania volunteers, they are an interesting
group because they're led by a guy named Christian

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.000
Yingling.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.710
And he played a pretty.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:24.570
He played a pretty notable role in Charlottesville
in 2017.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:30.420
And your listeners probably recognize that
that was the the white supremacist rally in

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.710
Charlottesville that also drew in a lot of
militia groups who said they were there to

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:40.810
basically keep the peace between right wing
protesters and left wing counterprotesters.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.220
Some people question that.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.120
So Yingling was there.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.960
He actually got sued and agreed to never come
back to Charlottesville with the militia.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.840
So he's he's been in the news.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.870
He's a very sort of colorful character.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.549
So I got to know him through that.

00:01:57.549 --> 00:02:01.920
And I had been doing a number of interviews
with him and trying to get in on his training

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.110
because I wanted to give listeners of my podcast,
Extremely American.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:12.620
I wanted to give them a real feel for what
it's like to be at a training with a militia,

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.870
what they do get there on the gun range with
them.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.569
So that's what brought me out there.

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:22.470
It took me a while to get their trust, but
they eventually let me come to their monthly

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:27.030
training, which is deep in the Pennsylvania
woods, kind of out in these rolling mountains

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.400
in western Pennsylvania, this sort of classic
site, you would imagine a militia training

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.520
happening at.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.340
Yeah, that's what that's what got me there.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.030
Speaker 1: And you know, we've spoken to people
who who have I don't want to use the term

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:42.709
infiltrated but who have been sort of like
granted access to different types of extremists

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:47.620
and different different journalists and reporters
take different approaches in terms of whether

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:52.770
they just remain extraordinarily neutral in
terms of their opinion of what it is that

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:56.800
they're observing or whether they are sort
of engaged in conversations about their views

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.550
as to what these groups do.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:04.250
What's the dynamic like when you're with these
folks in terms of how they see you and sort

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.320
of what the conversations are like?

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.260
Speaker 4: Yeah, I mean, so if you if you
if you listen to the podcast and especially

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:18.300
episode one with this group, what you'll hear
is I let them tell their own story.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.750
But you know, I don't I don't just sit on
the sidelines.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.190
If somebody says something outlandish, I challenge
them.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:24.190
Yeah.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:25.660
So I'll give you an example.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:29.560
You know, Christian Yingling at one point
he's talking about being between you went

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.040
to a Trump rally where they were counterprotesters
and he was standing with his guys between

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.500
the left and right wing protesters.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.100
Well, he talks about the story about how he
was basically trying to get the left wing

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.530
protesters to back up.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.600
Now they're in a public park, right?

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.320
He has no authority there.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:49.940
He's a private citizen and he's telling them
to back up and, you know, and he's telling

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.580
them, basically, if you don't back up, I'm
going to break your leg.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:53.930
So the guys?

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:57.900
And that's, you know, that's where I do step
in and I'm like, Well, now hold on.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.650
Under whose authority do you do that?

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:01.800
And so there's back and forth like that.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.930
I do want to let them tell their own story
because I think that's more interesting for

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.019
a listener than me just spouting off.

00:04:09.019 --> 00:04:13.540
But certainly, I'm not going to let something
like that just sort of twist in the wind.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:18.919
So there's some kind of fun back and forth
about that about January six, about Donald

00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:19.919
Trump.

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:24.219
So, yeah, you know, I'll challenge people
when I feel like that is appropriate.

00:04:24.219 --> 00:04:28.889
But I do also want people to hear their viewpoint
because I think understanding these groups

00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:33.189
is much more important than than slamming
them, for example, just to just to sort of

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:35.719
feel good
Speaker 1: at the individual level.

00:04:35.719 --> 00:04:41.169
You I've spoken to a lot of former hate group
members, including KKK and some other groups,

00:04:41.169 --> 00:04:47.059
and the one theme that is present in all of
their stories was they were sucked in because

00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:53.150
of a sort of personal vulnerability of their
situation that came often from sort of like

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:58.949
the prototypical absent or broken family or
lack of direction from parents.

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:03.240
It's like a very similar story that they all
tell.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:09.699
Is that a common back story in the folks that
you see in the militia groups or is that different?

00:05:09.699 --> 00:05:15.169
Speaker 4: So I would say there's a little
bit, a little bit of yes and no there, I've

00:05:15.169 --> 00:05:18.029
certainly met people who fit that mold.

00:05:18.029 --> 00:05:22.690
I think with militia groups, though, because
most of them, they're they're not explicitly

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:27.449
racist and a lot of them actually will at
least outwardly reject that.

00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:30.999
I think they have a bit of broader appeal,
I think.

00:05:30.999 --> 00:05:35.629
I think when you're talking about like explicitly
white supremacist groups, I think that recruitment

00:05:35.629 --> 00:05:39.949
is a little tougher and you really have to
to to try harder with the militia groups.

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:42.240
They try to tell, they try to have a bigger
tent.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.590
I guess you can say so.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:48.789
What I do find, though, is people who are
very disaffected with what's happening in

00:05:48.789 --> 00:05:49.800
America.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.710
They might be uncomfortable with a changing
country.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.649
They might be uncomfortable with increasing
diversity.

00:05:55.649 --> 00:05:59.569
They may not say it like that, but they will
talk about certain things like transgender

00:05:59.569 --> 00:06:03.080
rights come up a lot, and that makes a lot
of people in this movement uncomfortable.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:06.599
They'll talk about critical race theory now,
whether or not that's actually a real thing

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:07.770
in American schools.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.369
You know, that's obviously something that
people are debating.

00:06:11.369 --> 00:06:16.339
But there is a discomfort with the changing
America that draws people in.

00:06:16.339 --> 00:06:21.300
And I think certainly Donald Trump's rise
to power and some of the things he said, they

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.909
do feel more empowered than they have and
I think made him feel more comfortable coming

00:06:24.909 --> 00:06:28.419
out in the open as opposed to just training
out in the woods.

00:06:28.419 --> 00:06:29.990
Speaker 1: Is there?

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:33.800
Is there a notable undertone of anti-Semitism
in these groups?

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:39.210
Speaker 4: I would actually say no.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.060
I am a Jewish reporter.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:44.990
I, you know, I did not feel that directly.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:51.819
I did speak to one very prominent Jewish member
of the three percent militia in Washington

00:06:51.819 --> 00:06:52.819
state.

00:06:52.819 --> 00:06:56.280
He figures prominently in episode three, and
we talk a little bit about his background

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:57.349
now.

00:06:57.349 --> 00:07:00.309
Is that to say that there is not a strain
of anti-Semitism?

00:07:00.309 --> 00:07:01.309
No.

00:07:01.309 --> 00:07:04.350
I also ran into some really offensive stuff
where people would talk about it.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:07.979
They would compare COVID protocols to Nazi
ism now.

00:07:07.979 --> 00:07:09.150
Is that directly anti-Semitism?

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:13.270
We can debate that, but it's really very offensive,
I think, to a lot of people.

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:18.699
So I would say explicitly, No, you do get
that undercurrent with some people, and I

00:07:18.699 --> 00:07:21.289
think you do get some sort of explaining away
of stuff.

00:07:21.289 --> 00:07:29.050
Speaker 1: How prominent is the presence of
law enforcement, current or former and military

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.090
current and former in your experience in these
groups?

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:38.770
Speaker 4: I run into a lot of ex-military
in these groups as you run into a lot of people

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:43.420
who kind of are in the, you know, I wish I
would have served kind of mold.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.060
But you do run into a lot of veterans.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:47.689
You run into some law enforcement.

00:07:47.689 --> 00:07:49.080
It gets kind of murky there.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.339
There's certainly some law enforcement that
have been supportive.

00:07:52.339 --> 00:07:55.850
There's a whole sheriffs movement called the
Constitutional Sheriffs Movement, and they

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.719
have elected sheriffs all over the country.

00:07:57.719 --> 00:08:02.189
Joe Arpaio in Arizona was part of that, and
they're very friendly to militias.

00:08:02.189 --> 00:08:06.580
Now you also get law enforcement who are dead
set against them because they see their anti-government

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:09.090
rhetoric as also anti law enforcement.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.389
So it's a very mixed bag.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:15.479
But I would say the veteran part of it that
is a robust part of the military are the militia

00:08:15.479 --> 00:08:16.479
movement.

00:08:16.479 --> 00:08:20.650
Speaker 1: Do the militia groups get along
with each other or do they see each other

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:23.990
as competitors or they believe they aren't?

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:27.659
You know, do they have problems with the ideologies
of some of the other groups?

00:08:27.659 --> 00:08:30.240
Speaker 4: So that's a great question.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.400
This might not be surprising, but anti-government
movements are not very good at centralized

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:36.400
control.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:42.800
So there is a lot of bickering back and forth
and a lot of infighting and frankly, just

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:49.970
some some honest disagreements where you have
groups that absolutely will disavow another

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.520
group, including different three percent groups.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:58.520
There's a group in Georgia that has a Confederate
flag in their logo, and some of the more some

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:01.940
of the some of the other three percenters
in the West, you know, they'll look to them

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:04.950
and say, We absolutely want nothing to do
with those guys.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.930
They're racist and we reject them.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.340
So it's a mixed bag.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:14.460
They do come together over two things a radical
view of limited government and pretty much

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:18.970
absolutist gun rights and a restriction on
gun rights they see as a red line.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:22.440
I would say almost every militia group agrees
on those two things.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.260
Speaker 1: What is the ah, let's see how I
can phrase this what?

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:33.020
There's the fear among observers of some of
these groups that at some point they're going

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.770
to do like their big thing, you know, like
the.

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:38.890
You think about Jim Jones in Jonestown.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:43.650
And eventually there was like the mass suicide
of the nine hundred and fifty people in South

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:44.650
America.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.270
And it was sort of like the culmination of
the entire thing.

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:50.380
And there's the fear that some equivalent
not not killing themselves, necessarily, but

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:54.890
there's the equivalent that they're building
to some kind of big event.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:58.160
Do you get the sense that that's the eventual
goal?

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:05.020
Or are they there more of as like as a constant
low grade protection as they see it against

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:08.960
the tyranny and oppression of the government
or whatever the case may be without planning,

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:10.520
like some big event?

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:18.330
Speaker 4: I think what you're seeing is and
what I get into an extremely American is this

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:24.900
shift away from that kind of thinking more
to, hey, we have this moment where we've got

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.030
more mainstream support.

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.080
Let's try to change government from the inside.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:33.560
There's a lot of younger leaders who I would
say are much more politically savvy than the

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.470
old guard from the Michigan militia and those
kind of groups.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:40.630
And you know, they're they're targeting the
local school boards, their library boards.

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:46.020
They're picking off state Senate and state
legislature seats.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.080
So that's not to say we couldn't have that.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.080
I mean, we've seen it.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:50.520
We've seen it at least twice.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:54.570
We saw Cliven Bundy's ranch, where all these
militia guys were pointing weapons at federal

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.950
agents that carry guns quickly.

00:10:56.950 --> 00:11:01.750
And we saw it in Oregon, where Ammon Bundy,
Cliven son, had an armed standoff of his own.

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.340
Now we could totally see that again.

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:07.560
I think a lot of these guys are much more
focused on trying to bring government to the

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:14.820
right as opposed to having an armed confrontation,
even though when you ask them, they will admit

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:19.180
they are simultaneously training to fight
against the government and kind of trying

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:20.180
to be a part of it.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:21.180
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:23.720
Well, so that was going to be my next thing,
which is you talked about the presence of

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:25.900
military veterans.

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:30.840
There's in hypothetical discussions about
what might happen if one of these groups really

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:32.260
tried something big.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:37.350
There's the reality that eventually they might
be confronting not just police, but if they

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:38.990
really wreaked enough havoc.

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:44.070
The National Guard, or they would sort of
be against the military, but they are made

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:45.070
up.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.340
They have a significant former military presence.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:51.370
How do they conceive of that, of the idea
that ultimately the force that might stop

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:54.610
them is the one that many of them were once
a part of?

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:58.430
Speaker 4: Right, and that's something you
can't escape.

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:03.830
When I talked to militia members and leaders
about that, often they kind of deflects at

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:05.280
first.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.010
But when I pressed them, they admit yes.

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:13.410
I mean that you can't talk about fighting
the government without the possibility.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.830
And really, the almost probability if you
fight the government that your guns are going

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:21.220
to be pointed at law enforcement and or troops.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:25.990
Now what they'll say if you press them is,
you know, righteous righteous troops and law

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:30.390
enforcement will step aside because the battle
will be righteous for the militia guys.

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:33.290
But it's absolutely something that hangs in
the background.

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:34.290
And you can't avoid it.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:40.030
And when you look at those standoffs, they
were against law enforcement and they weren't

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.260
against troops that they were against law
enforcement.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:47.480
And in the case of the Oregon standoff, you
know, there was a deadly, deadly shootout

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:49.030
and a man got killed.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:51.660
So it's not it's not theoretical that could
happen.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:56.060
And it has happened
Speaker 1: in terms of the political alignment

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:59.290
when it comes to Democrats, Republicans, etc.

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:02.620
I mean, you made it clear they want to move
the government to the right.

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:07.430
Their most important issues certainly align
more with the Republican Party than the Democratic

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:08.430
Party.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.800
But like, is there any political diversity
within these groups?

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:16.970
Speaker 4: Yeah, and they also get into that
in the podcast because I think the political

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:20.440
angle on these groups, it makes them so much
more interesting now.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:22.740
I mean, I should say yes and no.

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:26.130
Are any of them Democrats that I've met?

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:27.300
No.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:29.240
Do they have diverse political views?

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:30.240
Yeah, yeah.

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.020
Yeah, there there is a diversity of views.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:35.110
Some are more libertarian, sort of live and
let live.

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:39.460
Some of them are really much more sort of
fundamentalist, far right groups.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:43.080
And I would say more of them are of that ilk.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:44.660
And that's why they're targeting school boards.

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:49.480
They talk a lot about critical race theory
and culture war issues.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:52.140
And I think that's a big change from the 1990s.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:56.940
In the 1990s, they might have been very conservative,
but they were very much pox on both parties.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:58.720
They're all the same.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:02.960
These guys today, they're much more involved
in Republican politics, and I think that's

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:08.030
a really important shift because they've sort
of pick sides in a way that they didn't in

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:09.030
the past.

